WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.015
Here is the FX rate cache from this lesson's lab.

2
00:00:06.015 --> 00:00:11.155
On a miss, it does a 100 ms socket read, while it holds the cache's monitor.

3
00:00:11.155 --> 00:00:15.349
In Kotlin, the Synchronized annotation makes the same monitor.

4
00:00:16.167 --> 00:00:20.569
The JDK runs virtual threads on carrier threads, one per core.

5
00:00:20.569 --> 00:00:21.919
Say there are four.

6
00:00:22.133 --> 00:00:24.006
VT 1 to 4 call rate.

7
00:00:24.006 --> 00:00:27.096
VT 5 and 6 never touch the cache.

8
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:32.488
VT 1 mounts on carrier 1, enters rate, and takes the monitor.

9
00:00:32.488 --> 00:00:38.016
On JDK 21 to 23, the JVM records a monitor's owner per OS thread.

10
00:00:38.016 --> 00:00:40.227
So the owner is carrier 1.

11
00:00:40.433 --> 00:00:42.647
Now VT 1 starts the socket read.

12
00:00:42.647 --> 00:00:46.174
A blocking read normally unmounts a virtual thread.

13
00:00:46.174 --> 00:00:50.393
But while VT 1 holds the monitor, it can't leave its carrier.

14
00:00:50.393 --> 00:00:53.367
It is pinned, and carrier 1 blocks with it.

15
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.013
VT 2, 3 and 4 mount and call rate.

16
00:00:56.013 --> 00:01:00.909
The monitor is taken, so each one blocks, and each one is pinned too.

17
00:01:01.133 --> 00:01:04.920
All four carriers are stuck, and the CPU does nothing.

18
00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:12.773
Four callers of rate have stopped every virtual thread in the JVM, even VT 5 and 6, which never touch the cache.

19
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:16.794
Now use a ReentrantLock instead.

20
00:01:16.794 --> 00:01:20.565
In Kotlin, withLock writes the try and finally for you.

21
00:01:22.167 --> 00:01:25.821
Again, VT 1 to VT 4 mount and call rate.

22
00:01:25.821 --> 00:01:28.196
VT 1 takes the lock first.

23
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:29.913
VT 1 starts the read.

24
00:01:29.913 --> 00:01:37.480
ReentrantLock parks the thread the normal way, so VT 1 unmounts to the heap, and it still holds the lock.

25
00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:40.173
VT 2, 3 and 4 ask for the lock.

26
00:01:40.173 --> 00:01:42.727
Each one parks and unmounts too.

27
00:01:42.933 --> 00:01:46.695
Now the carriers are free, and they run VT 5 and 6.

28
00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:52.779
When the rate arrives, VT 1 mounts again on a free carrier, stores the rate and unlocks.

29
00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:56.855
Then VT 2 gets the lock, and mounts on the last free carrier.

30
00:01:58.667 --> 00:02:01.312
JDK 24 fixed this with JEP 491.

31
00:02:01.312 --> 00:02:13.855
A virtual thread can now take, hold and release a monitor independently of its carrier, so synchronized and Object.wait unmount like ReentrantLock.

32
00:02:13.855 --> 00:02:17.695
JDK 25 is the first LTS release with the fix.

33
00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:22.407
So on JDK 25, leave working synchronized code alone.

34
00:02:22.407 --> 00:02:27.001
On JDK 21, put a ReentrantLock around blocking calls.
